Byte-for-byte identical to graph-eval-v1-postwp2.json / graph-eval-heldout-v2-postwp2.json
except for the `harnessVersion` field. That identity is the finding, and it is a measured one,
not an assumed one:

  - Liveness was proven separately with a temporary instrumented probe in `askContext` (since
    removed): the recall channel returns 5–6 hits per question with non-zero lexical scores and
    evidenceQuality 2, i.e. it genuinely produces an eligible seed set.
  - Identity of the two reports therefore means the recall channel's seed set on this corpus is
    REDUNDANT with the lexical channel's — it re-proposes the same nodes in the same order, so
    fusing it changes no ranking and no pack. `gd1` (the alpha-owned global decoy) is still
    excluded, but by S2's eligibility gate at the lexical/semantic channels, not by the recall
    fuse.

Consequence for the freeze (recorded, not tuned away)
------------------------------------------------------
The harness now exercises the third channel, so the S2 fuse is exercised and the gates-doc claim
is true as written. But on the frozen corpora the recall channel adds no discriminating power:
a regression that breaks ONLY the recall-channel eligibility gate would not move any gate value.
The discriminating proof for that gate therefore comes from a unit test, not from the corpora.

Added: `packages/mcp/src/verbs-memory-graph.test.ts` →
"the recall channel cannot seed a foreign-placed record at repo scope (S2 fuse is not vacuous)".
The test injects a foreign-placed (global) record at RANK 1 of the recall channel by stubbing
`memorySearch`, so no score floor can be what excludes it, then asserts a repo-scoped context
drops it while the same record IS admitted at global scope.

Discrimination was MEASURED, not asserted. With the pre-fix hole reproduced at the fuse call
site in `verbs.ts` (pre-WP2 `recallSeeds` admitted `typeof score === 'number'` with no
natural-zero floor, and the fuse had no eligibility gate, so every recall hit became a
distance-0 seed), the test FAILS:

  FAIL  src/verbs-memory-graph.test.ts > memory_graph WP2 placement laws (S2/S3) >
        the recall channel cannot seed a foreign-placed record at repo scope (S2 fuse is not vacuous)
  AssertionError: expected [ …(2) ] to not include 'mem:c373bb3bda4542ae518a18de4d951af3f…'
   ❯ src/verbs-memory-graph.test.ts:594:23

With the hole reverted the test passes; mcp suite 489 → 490/490, all 26 files. The temporary
emulation is NOT in the tree (`git diff packages/mcp/src/verbs.ts` is empty).

The suite's other placement tests were re-checked against the same emulation: none of them
discriminates (they pass either way — the decoy they use is dropped by a channel other than the
fuse), which is why this test had to be written rather than the gate assumed covered.

Corpus v3 should still add a fixture that makes the channel discriminating end-to-end (spec §9
gaps list).

Residue unchanged: v1 forbidden 3 = `g1` only, on the three `q-decoy-window-emptiness-{c,e,p}`
probes; emptiness 3 per corpus = the enumerated rows. GO remains gated on corpus v3.
